\begin{center}
  \textbf{Abstract}
\end{center}
\thispagestyle{empty}
\pagestyle{empty}
  This study presents analyses of variation in subject pronoun systems in the French and Creole of Creoles and Cajuns in and around Lafayette, Louisiana with a focus on how they navigate these systems to construct social meanings and ethnic identities.
  After several shifts for how Creoles and Cajuns have been defined over time, they are most often described today as Black and White South Louisianians.
  Not only are these local categories entangled with racialized categories of the broader US, but they are also entangled with language ideologies surrounding French and Creole.
  These language varieties themselves present a wide range of possible subject pronouns that have been attested, providing ample space for variation and meaning-making.

  In order to better understand how subject pronouns in French and Creole relate to and are used by Creoles and Cajuns, three analyses were carried out.
  First, variation in the subject pronoun choices of participants in French and Creole was put through a traditional quantitative variationist analysis to determine if Creoles' and Cajuns' subject pronouns varied systematically between each other.
  Second, homophily with respect to participants' identities as Creole or Cajun was examined in their personal social networks in an effort to get a more fine-grained understanding of subject pronoun variation.
  Third, a discourse analysis was carried out on the way Creoles and Cajuns employ subject pronouns relative to broader patterns of variation to construct identities and make social meaning.

  Subject pronouns were found to vary systematically between Creoles and Cajuns, but network homophily did not appear to act as a mechanism that entailed that variation.
  Creole-marked subject pronouns were exploited in discourse to index localness, closeness, and Blackness, whereas Cajun-marked subject pronouns indexed Whiteness and signaled various types of distance.

  This study provides new documentation of French and Creole grammatical systems while also providing insights into how these systems interact with the social worlds of Creoles and Cajuns.
  The present work furthermore adds to the relatively recent sociolinguistic literature that aims to better incorporate work on race in sociology into the field through the raciolinguistics paradigm.

